{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.QLommelOrderRecurrence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.QLommelOrderRecurrence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.double_root_linear","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.double_root_linear","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L114-L126","doc":"**Double-root / C-finite linear expansion.** The special case `r = s = 1`,\ncorresponding to the recurrence coefficient `b = 2`. The general solution\nis the linear sequence `a n = α + β * n`. This formalises the decisive C-finite\nrecurrence found in the extended alpha-cluster tower where `b_ν ≈ 2` and `ρ ≈ 1`\n(constant binding fractions). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.first_kind_geometric_b","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.first_kind_geometric_b","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L94-L112","doc":"**The recurrence coefficient depends only on the geometric RATIO, never on the\nnormalization** — the formal content of \"the b ladder is fixed by the exponent\nSLOPE alone\". For any offset `C` and any nonzero ratio `r`, the (pure first-kind)\ngeometric ladder `A ↦ C · rᴬ` solves the order recurrence with `b = r + r⁻¹`.\n\nWriting the binding residual as `Δ_λ(A) = C · (q₀¹⁰)ᴬ` — slope `10` (so\n`r = q₀¹⁰`, DERIVED from the A-only crossing count `σ+σ⁻¹ = 10A−2`, verified\n`A = 2..12` in `qlommel_b_ladder_slope_derivation.py`) — the coefficient is\n`b = q₀¹⁰ + q₀⁻¹⁰ = 2cosh(10 log q₀)` **regardless of the prefactor `C`**. The\nopen exponent OFFSET (the `−5` in `10A−5`, equivalently the `−3` crossing-vs-site\npuzzle) is exactly this `C = q₀⁻⁵`; it folds into the two seed constants\n(`two_seed_unique`) and therefore does NOT gate the recurrence. Hence the b ladder\nis derivable from the (derived) slope even while the offset remains open. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.two_root_geometric","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.two_root_geometric","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L74-L92","doc":"**Two-root (second-kind) geometric solution ⟹ DERIVED coefficient.** If\n`r · s = 1`, the two-sided geometric sequence `a n = α·rⁿ + β·sⁿ` solves the\norder recurrence with the *constant* coefficient `b = r + s` — derived, not\nfitted (`r, s` are the two roots of `t² − b·t + 1`).\n\nThis is the formal bridge for the binding `Δ_λ` ladder: the canonical residual\n`Δ_λ(K) ≈ q₀^(10A−5)` (`docs/audits/2026-06-09-delta-lambda-canonical-residual.md`)\nis geometric with ratio `r = q₀¹⁰`, so `s = r⁻¹ = q₀⁻¹⁰` and the **derived**\nrecurrence coefficient is `b = q₀¹⁰ + q₀⁻¹⁰ = 2cosh(10·log q₀)`. The `+β·sⁿ`\nterm is the **second-kind admixture** (Y_ν / K_ν branch) that carries the\nper-isotope deviations from the pure first-kind geometric law. The open keystone\nis a categorical derivation of the exponent `10A−5 = 5(2A−1)` — the `5` is the\nBring quintic — from the W_free vs W_atom crossing/jet bookkeeping; this lemma\nis the recurrence half that the exponent derivation feeds. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.two_seed_top","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.two_seed_top","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L67-L72","doc":"**Closed two-seed expansion at the first heavy level.** For an A = 2,3,4\ntower indexed `ν = 0,1,2`, the top value is fixed by the two seeds and the single\ninterior coefficient `b 0`: `a 2 = b 0 · a 1 − a 0`. This is exactly the\nderive-or-refute used on `Δ_λ` (predict ⁴He from {D, T} and the derived `b`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.two_seed_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.two_seed_unique","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L50-L65","doc":"**Tower from two seeds.** Two solutions of the *same* order recurrence (same\ncoefficient sequence `b`) that agree on the first two terms agree everywhere.\nThis is the structural reason a derived `b_ν` plus two seeds determines the whole\n`Δ_λ` ladder — the bootstrap that removes the need for the missing tower data. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.Δ_predict_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.Δ_predict_succ","kind":"theorem","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L45-L48","doc":"**One-step prediction.** Under the recurrence, the next term is fixed by the\ntwo previous terms and the (derived) coefficient — no free parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.IsOrderThreeTermRec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.IsOrderThreeTermRec","kind":"def","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QLommelOrderRecurrence.lean#L39-L43","doc":"The order three-term recurrence `a (n+2) = b n · a (n+1) − a n`\n(the q-Lommel / q-Bessel order contiguity (◇), as a relation on any sequence\n`a : ℕ → K` with coefficient sequence `b : ℕ → K`). ","signatureChunk":0}]}